WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
Dragon's 35th cargo flight targets Tuesday, sending a reused capsule toward the space station. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 127. Falcon 9 is on the clock for 6.33 AM Eastern on October 13th, lifting from SLC 40 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station, Space Coast Daily Reports. The backup window is Wednesday, October 14th at 608 AM Eastern. This Dragon already flew CRS-26, CRS-29, and CRS-33.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:36.420
After a roughly 52-hour trip, it is set to dock on its own Thursday, October 15, around

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.640
11 a.m. eastern.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.340
The first stage booster will be on its 8th flight and is targeted to land at Landing

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.440
Zone 40 after stage separation.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:56.640
Its earlier missions include KF-01, IMAP, NRL-77, GPS-3-9, CRS-34, NRL-95, and one Starlink

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.040
launch.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.420
Astronaut and Neil Menon shared video from inside a dragon of a sickness spacecraft in

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.400
a controlled departure.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:09.020
Maddie-Mam reports, the footage is sped up ten times and records a release he described

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.440
as slow and controlled.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.520
People watching called the sequence amazing and beautiful, and one of the reasons they love

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.540
space technology.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.940
Elon Musk posted on X, a social claim rather than a company filing, that he asked Grock-Bott

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.840
to research and order the most fun Lego Star Wars kids for young kids.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.980
The bot located options, compared prices, and finished checkout from an uploaded card

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.500
image with no further prompts.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.080
Musk said the purchase completed as easily as the request.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:43.060
Sci-Guy Space, in commentary rather than an official briefing, said eight months is a substantial

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.920
increment for spaceflight health research on the International Space Station, or ISS,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.540
not a short period.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:02:02.640
The account stress that station research with astronauts like Sophie help scientists understand and mitigate long duration health impacts and said SpaceX is not interested in the market amid talk of launch and satellite services.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:13.520
One thing worth watching is that SpaceX's spectrum acquisition for direct to sell still requires starship to reach a regular launch cadence before the plan can scale to a full mobile offering.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.720
The purchase moves the service from extra texting toward a carrier offering, yet what

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.140
flies today is still texting from coverage holes via T-Mobile, according to Big O.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.200
Carriers and regulators are still taking on transition risk, and the fresh satellites

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.640
and ground gear are not running at volume yet.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:36.520
Grush said industry chief executives have sold SpaceX short before, and would not forecast

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.040
how the carrier issue ends.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.160
From an engineering standpoint, heavy lift calendars are set by testing rules and contract

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:49.000
incentives, not by chamber pressure. All up testing means you fly the entire vehicle stack as

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.320
soon as subsystems can survive a real attempt. Taking more risk on that combined flight so the

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.180
loop from data to the next vehicle gets shorter. Saturn V went from a locked configuration to first

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.880
flight in about six years because every test article also had to serve as a flight article.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:11.520
There was no extra stack whose only job was to sit still and generate paperwork. Minimum viability

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.540
is engines that light, tanks that hold pressure, and guidance that can steer.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.760
Not a binder that says the system is already qualified.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.600
The missing qualification is the flight itself.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:27.240
Cost plus contracts invert that incentive by reimbursing hours and materials instead of

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.420
paying for a flight rate.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.020
Heritage parts get re-qualified again and again because the hours are billable, and the calendar

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.280
stretches without anyone being paid to fly.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:45.940
SpaceX's internal build and fly model removes a licensing layer that would otherwise sit between the people who designed the vehicle and the telemetry that comes off it.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:53.000
A licensing split turns telemetry into a report you throw over a wall, not a file the welder sees the next morning.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.260
Flight data can write the next hardware revision directly.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:03.060
You see that in booster flight counts that climb from mission to mission rather than resetting after each qualification campaign.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:08.520
On Starship, the same loop is only as fast as the time from a flight anomaly to hardware

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.180
on the next vehicle, with no outside contract gate in the middle.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.440
A second number sits under all of this, the idiot index, how many times the finished

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.260
super heavy booster or ship costs over the propellant, metal, and avionics inside it?

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:28.020
Call the raw bill the magic wand number, steel, propellant, and boxes, and nothing else.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:33.440
Coyle stainless is on the order of $3 a kilogram. Rocket caracene and liquid

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.160
oxygen sit near a 30 cents a kilogram. 90% of an orbital stage's mass is already

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.660
that cheap propellant so the metal you see on the pad is a thin shell around the

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:49.120
inexpensive part. If the finished stage then costs 50 times that raw bill. The index

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:55.220
is 50. Some historical expendable stages ran near 100. The gap is labor, tooling,

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:59.000
fueling, overhead, and every extra article you build but refuse to fly.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.020
All up testing collapses the index because the flight is the qualification run and the

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.160
data source, so the next article takes the fix without a separate test vehicle.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.600
Cost plus widens the index because each requalification cycle adds paid labor and months of calendar,

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.440
while the underlying material cost does not move.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.200
Starships campaign keeps the index lower by flying the same booster and ship designs repeatedly,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:28.120
So, each super heavy booster recovery or ship anomaly can cut the effective cost of

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:28.760
the next flight.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:34.160
The six-year Saturn V run shows the index can fall fast when national priority lines

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.320
up with all up flying.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:41.020
The SLS record shows the other outcome, when the contract pays for hours instead of flights.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:45.560
The third piece is feedback speed between flight data and a design change.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.760
Saturn V engineers could revise the next article within months because the same teams built

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:51.960
the hardware and flew it.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:57.660
Cost-plus programs insert review layers that put years between a flight and a fix, often

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.260
two to four years of boards and re-qualification.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:05.860
SpaceX keeps the teams and the data in one loop, so a booster that returns from flight can

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.700
inform the next build without an external licensing gate.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:14.540
That loop now runs on Starship, where telemetry on engines, heat shield and stage separation

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.400
feeds the following vehicle directly.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.760
The measurable outcome is a rising flight count on individual boosters and a shorter interval

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.920
between test articles. The reason you cannot skip the combined flight is physics, not culture.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:32.320
Pogo oscillation, staging shock, and engine out transients are stack-level behaviors.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.980
A component stand will not show you how the whole vehicle rings. Acoustic load at lift-off

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:43.020
couples through tanks, engines, and structure together. Staging is a collision you schedule.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.640
Thrust cuts, clamps release, allage fires, the upper stage lights, and a cloud you just made.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:53.440
You learn whether that sequence is stable only by doing it with flight weight tanks and

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:54.200
real engines.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.660
A heat shield's real environment is plasma on a returning ship, not a torch on a coupon.

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.900
Flying the stack early is how you sample those loads.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.160
You may lose hardware, you do not get the years back.

